-artificial-intelligence-bias">What is Artificial Intelligence Bias</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/who-is-the-father-of-artificial-intelligence">Who is the Father of Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/sop-for-ms-in-artificial-intelligence">SOP for MS in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/top-ai-penny-stocks-in-india">Top AI Penny Stocks in India</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-ai-in-data-analytics">What is AI in Data Analytics</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-business-management">Artificial Intelligence in Business Management</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/partial-order-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Partial Order Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-auditing">Artificial Intelligence in Auditing</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-construction">Artificial Intelligence in Construction</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/information-retrieval-in-artificial-intelligence">Information Retrieval in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-psychology">Artificial Intelligence in Psychology</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/iterative-deepening-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Iterative Deepening Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/applications-of-deep-learning-in-artificial-intelligence">Applications of Deep Learning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/7-artificial-intelligence-projects-in-agriculture">7 Artificial Intelligence Projects in Agriculture</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-multimodal-ai">What is Multimodal AI</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/benefits-of-artificial-intelligence-in-business">Benefits of Artificial Intelligence in Business</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conceptual-graphs-in-artificial-intelligence">Conceptual Graphs in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/problem-space-and-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Problem Space and Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conditional-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Conditional Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/informed-search-algorithms-in-artificial-intelligence">Informed Search Algorithms in Artificial Intelligence</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading10"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse10" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse10" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Related Tutorials</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading10" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse10"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tensorflow">Tensorflow Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch">PyTorch Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reinforcement-learning">Reinforcement Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="propositional-logic-inferences-in-artificial-intelligence">next →</a> <a class="next" href="customer-segmentation-with-llm">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Graph Search in Artificial Intelligence</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>2 Apr 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 12 min read</span></div><h2 class="h2">Introduction</h2><p>Graph search is an important approach in artificial intelligence that helps move through structures formed from interrelated data elements called nodes associated with edges. These structures, known as graphs, are applied in different fields to model relations in social networks, delivery systems, Web site navigation, video games, etc. Graph search algorithms can be described as the methods allowing AI systems to interact with these structures and find the solution. Therefore, such algorithms are critical in problem-solving and general decision-making.</p><p>In <a href="artificial-intelligence-ai">AI</a>, there are <a href="introduction-of-graphs">graphs</a> of use in circumstances where the relation is essential, such as pathfinding in a navigation system or clustering in a social network system. Verification and validation of these performances help to check whether AI systems become dependent on the choices of graph search algorithms that plan out search paths in a structured manner in the hope of arriving at the best solution.</p><p>Graph search algorithms are commonly used in any AI application, such as robot path planning, analysis of complex networks, NLP, and even in-game AI. Due to flexibility in various management situations, they are so helpful in solving problems involving relationship and connectivity issues. The advancement of efficient graph search algorithms will keep advancing AI's frontiers, especially as the environments become complex.</p><h2 class="h2">Critical Concepts in Graph Search</h2><h3 class="h3">Nodes and Edges</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Nodes (Vertices):</em></strong> Nodes in a graph that correspond to objects, states or any data.</li><li><strong><em>Edges:</em></strong> Links between the nodes as the links may represent relations between the nodes or as moves from one node to another, which may be either direct or bidirectional.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Graph Representation</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Adjacency List:</em></strong> One technique for modelling a graph where every node contains a list of adjacent nodes.</li><li><strong><em>Adjacency Matrix:</em></strong> A <a href="data-structure-2d-array">two-dimensional array</a>, with rows and columns of nodes being interrelated and cell values meaning connections.</li><li><strong><em>Edge List:</em></strong> A string of all defined edges in that graph in the form of the first one-joined node and the second-joined node.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Pathfinding</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Paths:</em></strong> Vectors of nodes with connections through edges; paths in a graph.</li><li><strong><em>Shortest Path:</em></strong> The shortest route that connects two nodes with the least sum of the links weights. Dijkstra's and Bellman-Ford are other types of algorithms used for pathfinding, likely to give the shortest one.</li><li><strong><em>Path Cost:</em></strong> The sum of the weight or cost of the traverse that is central to optimizing a particular route.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Heuristics</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Heuristic Function:</em></strong> An estimation technique that calculates the cost from any current node to the last or goal node that helps the focalised convocation search like A*.</li><li><strong><em>Admissible Heuristic</em>:</strong> That heuristic never comes up with solutions that are farther than the actual cost if the goal has to be reached.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Graph Search Properties</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Completeness:</em></strong> Tells whether a search algorithm will always find the solution in the event of a solution.</li><li><strong><em>Optimality:</em></strong> Refers to the idea that the discovered solution is optimal.</li><li><strong><em>Time Complexity:</em></strong> Evaluates the time complexity, generally expressed in terms of nodes expanded.</li><li><strong><em>Space Complexity:</em></strong> As such, it measures the memory usage of an algorithm about the nodes it stores during the search.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Traversal Techniques</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Graph Traversal:</em></strong> The systematic stepping through of each entity represented in a graph.</li><li><strong><em>Pre-order, In-order, and Post-order:</em></strong>Techniques that enable movement from one point in the tree structure, derived from graph theory mainly in the binary trees.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Connectivity</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Connected Graph</em>:</strong> A graph in which it is possible to find a direct connection between any two nodes it contains.</li><li><strong><em>Disconnected Graph:</em></strong> Connected components of a graph that are not affiliated with one another.</li><li><strong><em>Strongly Connected:</em></strong> In directed graphs, all the nodes are connected to all other nodes.</li><li><strong><em>Weakly Connected:</em></strong> In the case of directed graphs, nodes are reachable when the direction is not considered.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Weighted and Unweighted Graphs</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Weighted Graph:</em></strong> Graphs whose edges are labelled by the weights or costs used in problems involving cost optimisation.</li><li><strong><em>Unweighted Graph:</em></strong> Graphs in which all edges have the same values, exceptional cases in most of them what is relevant is the distance in the number of steps.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Breadth-First Search</h2><p><a href="breadth-first-search-algorithm">Breadth First Search (BFS)</a> is the algorithm that searches for nodes in a graph based entirely systematically and level by level. It takes a start node and then evaluates all the adjacent nodes, then it traverses through the neighbours of these neighbours and so on until it reaches the terminating node or an asked-for node. BFS is often used when the goal is to identify the neighbours of the start node.</p><h3 class="h3">How BFS Works?</h3><p>BFS uses a queue data structure to maintain nodes to follow.</p><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Initialization:</em></strong> Insert the start node into a queue and set its status as visited.</li><li><strong><em>Processing Nodes:</em></strong> Remove the first element of the queue or pop the first element of the queue. We wanted to complete some tasks on the node, such as printing it or checking printing it or checking if it is equal to the target.</li><li><strong><em>Enqueue Neighbours:</em></strong> Enqueue all other neighbours who have not yet visited and mark them as visited.</li><li><strong><em>Repeat:</em></strong> Keep calling the dequeue node and enqueueing the neighbouring nodes until the QUEUE is empty or the node of interest is found.</li></ul><p>This ensures that BFS can investigate all the nodes nearest the starting node before adding nodes farther from the starting node.</p><h2 class="h2">What BFS is used for?</h2><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Shortest Path in Unweighted Graphs:</em></strong> Again, BFS is the method of choice for identifying the shortest path between two nodes when the weight of the edges is the same.</li><li><strong><em>Connectivity Checking:</em></strong> An application of BFS is to decide whether all graph nodes are accessible from a particular node.</li><li><strong><em>Level Order Traversal:</em></strong> BFS is a method to traverse levels in trees, which can be helpful when dealing with hierarchical data.</li><li><strong><em>Network Analysis:</em></strong> It plays a significant role in social network analysis to know whether it is connected or the number of steps away we are from someone else in a network.</li><li><strong><em>Search in Mazes or Grids:</em></strong> It is used to get out of mazes and find ways out on maps laid out in grids.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Depth First Search</h2><p>Depth-first search (DFS) traverses a graph where the entire tree is traversed as far as possible before moving back to the other. It has a backtracking nature to solve all nodes or prove whether a graph has a sought target. It is combined with the deep first search when searching deeper stages of a problem or a solution that may be concealed deep into the search graph.</p><h3 class="h3">How DFS Works?</h3><p>DFS uses a stack data structure to store nodes either directly using space at the top or indirectly using recursion.</p><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Initialization:</em></strong> Choose an arbitrary node, set it as visited and add it to the stack (or pass it as a parameter).</li><li><strong><em>Exploring Nodes:</em></strong> The first node we remove from the stack and consider is now we proceed to the next step. As a target node, if the current node is reached, stop it.</li><li><strong><em>Push Neighbours:</em></strong> Pop a node from your stack; if it is not visited, mark it visited then push every one of its unvisited neighbors into the stack.</li><li><strong><em>Backtracking:</em></strong> If no such node exists, backtracking is done through the nodes stored in the stack and treated as previous levels.</li><li><strong><em>Repeat:</em></strong> Tackle this one until either the stack is exhausted or a solution to the goal has been discovered.</li></ul><p>This way enables DFS to go deep down one path before it widens its search to other paths.</p><h3 class="h3">What DFS is used for?</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Pathfinding in Mazes or Puzzles</em>:</strong> DFS is widely used for problems that involve going deeply into a path, such as mazes, puzzles and others.</li><li><strong><em>Cycle Detection in Graphs:</em></strong> In DFS, if a directed or undirected graph node is visited, it can find cycles.</li><li><strong><em>Topological Sorting:</em></strong> In dependency resolution DAGs, DFS is used to order the task because this algorithm is topological.</li><li><strong><em>Component Identification:</em></strong> DFS finds the distinct connected components in connected graphs.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Informed Search Algorithm</h2><p>Informed Search Algorithms are one of the categories of search techniques in artificial intelligence. The search space has to be enhanced by an extra knowledge of the problem-solving area to search for a goal much more quickly and efficiently. This knowledge is usually represented as a heuristic function that defines the cost estimate or distance to the goal so that these algorithms can concentrate on this work.</p><h3 class="h3">How the Search Engines Function?</h3><p>Priority-based memory selection algorithms in informed search provide a criterion to determine the next node to investigate.</p><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Initialization:</em></strong> Start with a state given this state to the heuristic function and compare it with the goal state. This state should be placed in a priority queue, also known as an open list, and ordered by the value of the heuristic.</li><li><strong><em>Node Selection:</em></strong> The process of selecting which node has to be removed from the open list for exploration is called backpropagation, and it means that the node with the most significant priority (the smallest value of the heuristic function) is chosen.</li><li><strong><em>Expansion:</em></strong> Produce all issues that might arise from the current node to arrive at the next node. Calculate each successor's heuristic value and put it into the priority queue.</li><li><strong><em>Goal Test:</em></strong> It should be checked whether the current node is the goal state. If so, stop and return the path to the goal or speak about it.</li></ul><p>Repeat this process until a goal state is possibly achieved or all the nodes are stimulated to the last level.</p><h3 class="h3">What is it that Informed Search Algorithms?</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Pathfinding:</em></strong> Such algorithms as the A* can be considered the best for pathfinding for navigation systems, robotics and game artificial intelligence.</li><li><strong><em>Optimization Problems:</em></strong> Such algorithms are used in logistics, resource assignment and timing issues.</li><li><strong><em>Complex Game Solving:</em></strong> Hinder algorithms assist in those games, such as chess, offering direction towards victories.</li><li><strong><em>Route Planning:</em></strong> Examples include creating a travel route map or distributing goods/ services.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">A* Search Algorithm</h2><p>A* Search Algorithm is one of the informed searching techniques in artificial intelligence which is more famous for searching the shortest path in a graph. This is a combination of <a href="dijkstras-algorithm">Dijkstra's algorithm</a> shortest paths and Greedy Best-First Search speed by employing a heuristic.</p><h3 class="h3">How A* Search Works?</h3><p><strong><em>Initialization:</em></strong> Begin from elementary status (the start vertex). The first data structure, the priority queue, more frequently called an open list, is created and contains the start node. This queue is a priority based on the estimated total cost of the nodes.</p><p><strong><em>Cost Calculation: A* uses two essential functions</em></strong></p><ul class="points"><li><strong>g(n):</strong> The amount of money spent getting to node n from the beginning of the network.</li><li><strong>h(n):</strong> The heuristic function that evaluates the cost from n to the goal state.</li><li><strong>A*</strong> combines these into a total cost function.</li><li>So the new equation is f(n) = g(n) + h(n), which represents the sum of estimates of indirect costs, g(n), for a time n to attain a set goal and h(n), which is other expenses for the same time n.</li></ul><p><strong><em>Node Expansion:</em></strong> Labyrinth chooses the node with the lowest f(n) value on the open list for expansion. This is done by making this particular node to be the current node.</p><p><strong><em>Successor Generation:</em></strong> Create successors of the current node. For each successor</p><p>Determine the g(n) and h(n) of this.</p><p>This should then add the new list and the total f(n) score to the open list.</p><p>Nodes need to be marked when they are discovered, and this can only be possible through the usage of what is known as a closed list.</p><p><strong><em>Goal Test:</em></strong> If any generational node meets the target, the process terminates, and the route to the goal is retraced.</p><p><strong><em>Repeat:</em></strong> Proceed with the process in a loop until one of the two conditions is met. The goal node has been found, or all the nodes in the open list have been exhausted.</p><h3 class="h3">What is A* used for?</h3><ul class="points"><li><strong><em>Navigation and Pathfinding:</em></strong> When used in a mapping system, robotics, or video games to determine an optimal path between two points.</li><li><strong><em>Puzzle Solving:</em></strong> In problems such as 8-puzzle or 15-puzzle to define the optimal path length towards the goal.</li><li><strong><em>Network Routing:</em></strong> Used in the routing protocols to find the best ways in networks.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Applications of Graph Search in AI</h2><h3 class="h3">Pathfinding in Robotics</h3><p>One of the critical areas where path search algorithms are indispensable is <a href="what-is-robotics">robotics</a> because of navigation and movement computation. For navigation, robots employ paths or techniques such as A* or Dijkstra to avoid contact with obstacles and get most quickly and safely from one point to another. These algorithms treat a map or grid as a graph, and nodes are the locations, while edges represent possible movement so that the robots learn how to move on their own.</p><h3 class="h3">Natural Language Processing</h3><p>Graph search finds applications in NLP for syntax parsing, machine translation and information extraction and extraction. For instance, in dependency parsing, words of a sentence are taken as nodes while the grammatical relation between them is taken as edges. Algorithms actively search these graphs to detect sentence structure, context or the optimal paths for translation, thus enhancing the understanding and processing of language.</p><h3 class="h3">Social Network Analysis</h3><p>From a social networking perspective, social networking dots are modelled as a graph where end users are the nodes while links (which may be friends or interactions) are the edges. The graph search algorithms prove helpful in analyzing these networks to identify influential users and communities or propose suitable connections. This analysis helps distinguish the areas of common interest and later market them, the interaction between people in social research, and in fraud detection cases.</p><h3 class="h3">Game AI</h3><p>Some activities involve in-game decision-making and formulation of strategies applying graph search algorithms. For board games, the current state of the game can be graphed; in each game, the player makes one move and ends up at a state that is another node. Strategies such as Minimax, at times in combination with a graph search can consider potential future moves to choose which path is the best, thus increasing AI wisdom of gameplay.</p><h3 class="h3">Knowledge Representation and Reasoning</h3><p>Graph search used in AI systems that use knowledge graphs (like semantic networks) helps search for information, relations, and search solutions where it is usually asked questions. Recommendation systems, question answering systems, and ontology management, these AI applications employ these algorithms to search and find their way through large complex networks of data. This makes it possible for AI systems to mimic the reasoning over large data sets.</p><h3 class="h3">Computer Vision</h3><p>In computer vision, graph search techniques have been used in identifying objects, segmenting images and comprehending scenes. Junctions within an image may be perceived as nodes, and algorithms such as graph cuts are valuable for identifying objects or boundaries between them. They help find focus areas commonly used in face detection and medical imaging and guide self-driving cars to navigate.</p><h2 class="h2">Advanced Graph Search Techniques</h2><h3 class="h3">Bidirectional Search</h3><p>Bidirectional Search is an optimization of the conventional search process. Rather than operating in the classical search mode from a given initial position (start node) towards a desired state (the goal), it conducts two parallel searches one towards the goal and the other towards the start node. Upon the occurrence of the two searches, the shortest route is obtained. This method cuts down the steps by a more significant proportion than the amount of time, making it applicable in path-finding in large networks or maps. This can be used, for example, in navigation systems and for directing the flow in a computer network.</p><h3 class="h3">Iterative Deepening Search (IDS)</h3><p><a href="iterative-deepening-search-or-iterative-deepening-depth-first-search">Iterative Deepening Search</a> is a search algorithm that enjoys both the space efficiency of DFS and the completeness of BFS. IDS operates by doing multiple DFS calls with increasing depth until a solution is found. This approach is of most value in cases where it is unclear how deep the solution is. Thus, the algorithm does not get trapped in the endless branches while still discarding the undesirable paths in search of the optimal solution. This is regularly used in GAI and situations where every byte of memory must be optimized to the fullest.</p><h3 class="h3">Heuristic Search</h3><p>Heuristic Search looks for a solution by using information already available in the problem area to assess the Nearness of nodes to the goal. Hence, similar to the Best-First Search, it provides a heuristic function on nodes that should lead to the goal of minimizing the number of nodes to search. This has made it faster than blind search algorithms such as the BFS. Such methods as A* (A-star) are an example of heuristics-based search often used in route planning, robotics and solving puzzles since they are fast in finding the shortest paths.</p><h2 class="h2">Conclusion</h2><p>In Conclusion, graph search is an area of artificial intelligence, is an example of a pattern problem-solving approach to decision-making in large problem domains. The graph search techniques provide multiple possibilities for solving AI problems, from the most straightforward techniques, such as BFS and DFS, to more complex ones, such as A* and MCTS. These algorithms are used in various areas, including path-finding, game artificial intelligence, network optimization, and optimisation strategic planning. The graph search methods are thus far from being replaced by new AI and remain essential for approaching more complex problems in different fields.</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="propositional-logic-inferences-in-artificial-intelligence">Propositional Logic Inferences in Artificial Intelligence</a></span></div><br/><br/><div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="customer-segmentation-with-llm" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="propositional-logic-inferences-in-artificial-intelligence" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br/><br/></td></tr></tbody></table></div></body></html><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="text-generation-using-gated-recurrent-unit-networks"><h3 class="h3">Text Generation using Gated Recurrent Unit Networks</h3><p class="related-post-content">What are Gated Recurrent Unit Networks? A Gated Recurrent Unit Network is a Recurrent Neural Network alternative to Long Short-Term Memory Networks (LSTM). The GRU can work on sequential data like text, speech, and time series. The core idea behind GRU is to employ gating techniques to selectively...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 8 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor"